      <h2>How to Choose a Coworking Space</h2>

      <h3>Test Before Committing:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Buy a day pass and spend a full day at the space</li>
        <li>Test the WiFi yourself (bring your laptop)</li>
        <li>Chat with members about their experience</li>
        <li>Check noise levels and atmosphere</li>
        <li>Verify meeting room availability and quality</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Questions to Ask:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What's the WiFi speed, and is it guaranteed?</li>
        <li>Is there a backup internet connection (4G/5G)?</li>
        <li>What meeting rooms are available, and at what cost?</li>
        <li>Are there community events or networking opportunities?</li>
        <li>Can I use the space address for business registration?</li>
        <li>What are the cancellation terms for monthly memberships?</li>
        <li>Are utilities, parking, or other costs included?</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Red Flags:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>WiFi slower than 50 Mbps (unsuitable for video calls or uploads)</li>
        <li>No backup internet option</li>
        <li>Shared desks in noisy open plans (if you need focus)</li>
        <li>Poor reviews on reliability or wifi consistency</li>
        <li>High turnover of members (suggests dissatisfaction)</li>
        <li>Long-term contracts without trial periods</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Alternative to Full Coworking Memberships</h2>

      <p><strong>Day passes:</strong> Perfect for nomads alternating home/coworking. Buy 5–10 days/month (€60–150) instead of a full membership (€200–350).</p>

      <p><strong>Hot desking:</strong> Many spaces offer cheaper "hot desking" (flexible desk sharing) vs. dedicated desks. Saves 30–50% if you're part-time.</p>

      <p><strong>Virtual offices:</strong> Some spaces offer mail handling and video call backdrops without a desk membership (€30–80/month).</p>

      <h2>Internet Reliability Matters</h2>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ</span>
        <p><strong>Home vs Coworking:</strong> Spain's home fibre is excellent, but coworking spaces with redundancy (dual connections) are more reliable for critical work. If your job requires zero downtime, coworking is worth the investment.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Ask coworking spaces about their uptime SLA (Service Level Agreement). Professional spaces guarantee 99.5%+ uptime.</p>

      <h2>Community & Networking Value</h2>

      <p>Beyond workspace, coworking offers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Networking with other remote workers and entrepreneurs</li>
        <li>Professional development events and workshops</li>
        <li>Reduced isolation (important for solo remote workers)</li>
        <li>Accountability and structure in your work environment</li>
        <li>Potential collaborations or business partnerships</li>
      </ul>

      <p>For many nomads, these intangible benefits justify coworking costs as much as the desk itself.</p>

